You've run into a common issue with rich text editors. Quill is trying to be helpful by "sanitizing" your input. When it sees a <span> with font-weight: bold, it converts it into a <strong> tag, which is the standard semantic HTML for bold text.
To prevent this and ensure your tag is treated as a special, non-editable element, you must define it as a custom Quill blot. This tells the editor to treat your variable as a single, atomic unit and not to interfere with its structure.
Here is the correct, robust solution.
1. Define the Custom Blot
First, create a custom "blot" that describes your variable tag. This tells Quill how to render and manage it. Place this code outside your component, near your other Quill imports.
// 💡 Define the custom element for your variables
const Embed = Quill.import('blots/embed');

class VariableBlot extends Embed {
  // This method is called when a new blot is created
  static create(value) {
    // Create the base DOM node
    const node = super.create();
    // Use the value (e.g., "{{FIRM_NAME}}") as the text
    node.innerText = value;
    // Important: Make it non-editable
    node.setAttribute('contenteditable', 'false');
    // Apply your custom styles
    Object.assign(node.style, {
      backgroundColor: '#0c476e',
      color: 'white',
      padding: '5px 8px',
      borderRadius: '3px',
      fontWeight: 'bold', // This is now safe inside a blot
      margin: '0 2px',
      cursor: 'default',
      userSelect: 'none',
    });
    return node;
  }

  // This is used to get the value back from the blot
  static value(domNode) {
    return domNode.innerText;
  }
}

// Register the blot with a unique name and the tag to use
VariableBlot.blotName = 'variable';
VariableBlot.tagName = 'span'; // Use a <span> tag for rendering

2. Register the New Blot
You must register your blot with Quill before the editor initializes. The best place for this is in a useEffect hook.
// Inside your EmailTemplate component

useEffect(() => {
  // ✅ Register our custom blot so Quill knows what 'variable' means
  Quill.register(VariableBlot);
  fetchData();
}, []); // The empty array ensures this runs only once on mount

3. Update handleDrop to Use the Blot
Finally, change your handleDrop function to insert your registered blot using editor.insertEmbed(). This is the correct method for adding non-textual or atomic content.
// Replace your entire handleDrop function with this one

const handleDrop = (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  if (!isEditing) return;

  const editor = bodyEditorRef.current?.getEditor();
  if (!editor) return;

  const text = e.dataTransfer.getData("text");
  const range = editor.getSelection(true);

  if (range) {
    // Insert the blot by its registered name ('variable')
    // and pass the dragged text as its value
    editor.insertEmbed(range.index, 'variable', text, Quill.sources.USER);

    // Move the cursor immediately after the new tag for a smooth UX
    editor.setSelection(range.index + 1, Quill.sources.USER);
  }
};

By implementing this, Quill will no longer convert your tag. It will be treated as a special variable element that you've defined, preserving its <span> tag and styles correctly.
